    "graph_img": "graph_png/hoyle_charleston_audit_graph.png",
    "posts": [
      "Artistic empathy as a bridge to historical trauma. A performance piece attempting to inhabit the lived experience of a massacre survivor.\n\nIn Charleston, I had the profound experience of embodying Polly Sheppard\nEvidence: shared humanity, artistic witness, healing resonance",
      "The claim is that theater can accurately and ethically represent the trauma of others, providing a form of communal healing and validation for the survivor.\nStated Judgement: (+1.0, +1.0) — Greater Good",
      "The reality is that the survivor herself validated the performance, suggesting the artistic transmission successfully bridged the gap between performer and subject.\nResulting Judgement: (+0.5, +1.0) — Lesser Good",
      "Verdict: PASS — The Path of Grace.\nThe performance successfully navigated the delicate boundary of representation without exploiting the subject's trauma.",
      "Dan Hoyle's performance piece involves embodying real-life figures to explore complex social issues. Here, he portrays Polly Sheppard, a survivor of the 2015 Mother Emanuel AME Church shooting.",
      "The Bright Side:\nTrue artistic witness can transform private suffering into a public narrative that fosters genuine empathy rather than voyeurism.",
      "The Breakdown & Plane Error:\nThere is no plane error here. The WHO (performer) and the WHO (subject) achieved alignment through the HOW (performance), creating a resonance that validates the WHY (meaning).",
      "The Trajectory: The Path of Grace.\nWhen you map the gap between the initial risk of appropriation and the actual reception by the survivor...",
      "...it plots a direct trajectory toward Lesser Good, where the expansion of empathy outweighs the inherent risks of theatrical representation.",
      "The Unavoidable Truth: Authentic witness requires the subject's consent and participation to be valid.\n\nThe Unavoidable Lie: That all stories are equally ours to tell without deep, personal engagement.",
      "Alethekanon:\nThis is a rare instance where the artistic process respects the causal history of the subject. By centering the survivor's own validation, the performance avoids the trap of performative empathy.",
      "Awwthekanon:\nIt is a beautiful thing when a survivor feels truly heard. This performance seems to have offered Polly Sheppard a moment of peace and recognition, which is the highest goal of any story.",
      "Brothekanon:\nUsually, actors trying to play real people is a recipe for cringe. But if the person who actually lived through it says you got it right, then you actually got it right. Respect."
    ],
